1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,660
Yesterday in Texas, a white sports car drove onto an upside-down track.

2
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:09,680
It drove across upside down, flipped over, and landed.

3
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:13,780
Then it "flew" over the roofs of five cars.

4
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:18,190
Musk said: this is the actual car that will be on the roads.

5
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:22,760
The internet split: real flight, or a very stylish jump?

6
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:28,690
Today we don't take sides. We do the math. Eight minutes to take this car apart.

7
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:34,050
Meet today's star: R, a slightly nervous sports car.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,270
Trick one: driving upside down, why doesn't it fall?

9
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:43,640
The answer is underneath: fans pump the air out from under the car.

10
00:00:43,590 --> 00:00:52,000
Thin air below, the whole atmosphere pressing above: the difference pins the car to the road.

11
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:55,510
It's called a fan car, and it isn't new.

12
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:01,740
In 1978, an F1 car with a giant fan on its back raced once, and won.

13
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:07,510
Then it was pulled. Rivals called it a vacuum cleaner that sprayed stones behind it.

14
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:15,370
Here's the key: wings need speed to make downforce, fans don't. Even standing still, the car sticks.

15
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,920
So once the suction beats the car's weight, the ceiling becomes the floor.

16
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:26,020
Tesla's screen showed about 4,700 kg of downforce, maybe more than twice the car's weight.

17
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:35,380
Even tech press suspect it's inflated. Another fan car, about a ton, claims two tons, which is already wild.

18
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:38,350
Trick one gets a score: the real thing.

19
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:42,810
Trick two: zero to sixty in one second.

20
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:51,090
Do the math: 96 km/h in one second is 27 meters per second, every second. About 2.7 g.

21
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:57,590
But a car pushes off the road through its tires, and even great tires top out at one-point-something g.

22
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,190
So what now? Two tricks.

23
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:04,400
First, the fans again: the harder the car is pressed down, the better the tires grip.

24
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:10,000
Second, more brutal: don't rely on the road at all. Blow gas out the back.

25
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:14,950
That's how a rocket works: throw stuff backward, and you go forward.

26
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:23,800
Reportedly, the four rear jets make about 80 kilonewtons. For a two-ton car, that alone is 4 g.

27
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:28,490
No independent measurement yet, so that 'one second' gets a question mark.

28
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:33,460
Trick three, the main event: how long can it actually fly?

29
00:02:33,410 --> 00:02:41,020
It doesn't burn anything. It blows compressed air: Tesla says 600 liters feed ten jets.

30
00:02:40,970 --> 00:02:44,920
600 liters sounds like a lot? It's about three bathtubs.

31
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:53,820
Tesla didn't give the pressure. Assuming a typical high-pressure tank, that's roughly 200 to 300 kilograms of air.

32
00:02:53,770 --> 00:03:01,730
Cold air jets out at a few hundred meters per second at most. For 80 kN, you burn over 100 kilos every second.

33
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:08,840
Two or three hundred kilos, over a hundred per second: at full blast, the thrust is mostly gone in a second or two.

34
00:03:08,990 --> 00:03:17,820
What about just hovering? Holding up two tons takes 20 kN, eating over 30 kilos of air a second.

35
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:23,050
And as it empties, pressure and thrust drop, until it can't hold the car.

36
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,520
There's also an invisible enemy: cold.

37
00:03:25,470 --> 00:03:32,720
Expanding gas gets cold. Hold down a spray can and it turns icy. Same thing.

38
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:40,890
A colder tank loses pressure even faster, and nozzles can frost up. So real airtime would be even shorter than our estimate.

39
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:45,050
Don't believe us? We built a virtual lab. Try it yourself.

40
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:49,770
300 atmospheres, hover at three meters: about five seconds.

41
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:55,100
Squeeze it to 700: about ten seconds.

42
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:01,000
Then cut 500 kilos off the car: just over twelve.

43
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:07,680
So the verdict: it doesn't fly. It does a very expensive jump.

44
00:04:08,130 --> 00:04:13,930
Still, a stylish jump takes real skill. How does it flip in mid-air?

45
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:20,870
The secret: the jets don't point through the center of mass. Off-center thrust turns the car. That's torque.

46
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:28,260
Fire the front but not the back, and the nose lifts. Fire the left but not the right, and it rolls.

47
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:35,240
Ten jets working together let it steer in the air. Rockets that land themselves use the same trick.

48
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:42,860
A brain-burner: a helicopter holds up two tons for an hour. Why do jets last only seconds?

49
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:50,190
Key: thrust is air times speed, but the energy you spend is air times speed squared.

50
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:58,150
Blasting a little air very fast wastes energy. Gently pushing lots of air is efficient.

51
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:08,490
A big rotor gently pushes a huge column of air. For the same two tons, small jets need about thirty times the power of a ten-meter rotor.

52
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:14,340
That's why the companies actually building flying cars all put propellers on them.

53
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:20,250
Trick three gets a score: right physics, but it's a stunt, not transportation.

54
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:23,260
OK, suppose you're sitting in it.

55
00:05:23,210 --> 00:05:28,790
Launching at 4 g is like the wildest moment on a roller coaster, and then some.

56
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:37,180
Upside down, blood rushes to your head. Tech press ask: will you need a fighter pilot's g-suit?

57
00:05:37,130 --> 00:05:42,700
And inside: every spare gap is packed with carbon-fiber-wrapped high-pressure tanks.

58
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:49,030
They're light and strong, but this type of tank has been linked to SpaceX rocket failures.

59
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:56,570
And a sum: filling those 600 liters takes, ideally, over 20 kWh, enough to drive this car over 100 km.

60
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:03,110
And bystanders: reportedly, the blast threw gravel and sand tens of meters during the demo.

61
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:06,920
So these stunts belong on a closed course.

62
00:06:06,870 --> 00:06:13,170
Musk himself said safety is not the main goal, and they will 'aspire not to kill anyone'.

63
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:16,310
Those are his words. No comment from us.

64
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,260
So what is this car, really?

65
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:24,140
The suction: real, around since the seventies, now turned up.

66
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:28,180
The one-second sprint: physically plausible, awaiting independent tests.

67
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:32,910
The flying: a few seconds of show. Cool, but it won't fly you to work.

68
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:41,970
Timeline: production in 2028. It was first unveiled in 2017 and delayed again and again.

69
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:47,450
One last easter egg: Musk said it might jump the Snake River Canyon.

70
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:55,240
In 1974 a stuntman tried it on a rocket bike. His parachute opened early, and he drifted into the canyon.

71
00:06:55,190 --> 00:06:59,310
If R actually makes it, we'll make another episode.

72
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:13,060
Note: the suction, the one-second sprint and 600 liters are Tesla's claims; 80 kN is from press reports; air mass, airtime and energy are our own estimates.

